1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It has revolutionized the way we work, play, travel, and communicate.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
It touches almost every part of our lives.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
It has helped win wars, solve insoluble problems, and send us into space.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:21,000
Its invention is the story of squandered chances, fortunate accidents,

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
frequent missteps, and unprecedented genius.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:30,000
Now the creation of the computer on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:52,671 --> 00:00:58,200
Our world is increasingly filled with countless wonders that would not have been possible without

8
00:00:58,201 --> 00:01:00,540
one machine, the computer.

9
00:01:00,640 --> 00:01:02,900
Although computers are enormously intricate,

10
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,940
their most basic components consist of simple devices

11
00:01:06,040 --> 00:01:09,980
that can be switched to either one of two states, on or off.

12
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:12,740
The computer creates its magic by calculating

13
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,480
with a speed and accuracy that far surpasses

14
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:17,760
its human inventors.

15
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,660
Computers stagger the mind with their complexity.

16
00:01:20,760 --> 00:01:24,360
But simply put, a computer takes information, processes it,

17
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:26,160
and then outputs a result.

18
00:01:26,161 --> 00:01:31,560
It's all done with a unique partnership of hardware and software.

19
00:01:31,660 --> 00:01:33,200
Hardware comes in boxes.

20
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:35,700
They're the physical components, such as the monitor

21
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:37,200
and hard drive.

22
00:01:37,300 --> 00:01:39,480
Software comes on disks.

23
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:41,440
Software consists of instructions

24
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:43,820
that tell the computer what to do.

25
00:01:43,920 --> 00:01:46,420
One way to start this computational partnership

26
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:49,520
is to type on the keyboard, providing input.

27
00:01:49,620 --> 00:01:52,480
The input is picked up by the central processing unit,

28
00:01:52,580 --> 00:01:56,160
or CPU, the computer's brain.

29
00:01:56,161 --> 00:01:58,540
Using instructions provided by software,

30
00:01:58,541 --> 00:02:01,060
the CPU processes the input.

31
00:02:01,061 --> 00:02:04,900
The magic behind the CPU is its blinding speed.

32
00:02:04,901 --> 00:02:07,800
Modern processors are measured in MIPS,

33
00:02:07,801 --> 00:02:10,440
millions of instructions per second.

34
00:02:10,441 --> 00:02:13,160
While processing, the CPU may receive data stored

35
00:02:13,161 --> 00:02:15,820
in random access memory known as RAM,

36
00:02:15,821 --> 00:02:18,480
or data stored on hard drive.

37
00:02:18,481 --> 00:02:20,800
Modern RAM is so quick that every second

38
00:02:20,801 --> 00:02:23,820
it can send the equivalent of 10,000 typewritten pages

39
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:26,760
of information to the CPU.

40
00:02:26,761 --> 00:02:30,860
And modern hard drives can store the equivalent of 250,000 pages

41
00:02:30,861 --> 00:02:33,180
of typewritten material.

42
00:02:33,181 --> 00:02:36,300
After processing the CPU outputs information, often

43
00:02:36,301 --> 00:02:40,480
on a monitor, the whole procedure is usually so quick

44
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:43,800
that it appears instantaneous.

45
00:02:43,801 --> 00:02:45,780
Today, computers are so commonplace,

46
00:02:45,781 --> 00:02:47,300
we take them for granted.

47
00:02:47,301 --> 00:02:49,780
But not long ago, computers only existed

48
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:53,160
in the imagination of a few visionaries.

49
00:02:53,820 --> 00:02:55,860
that could figure quickly and accurately

50
00:02:55,960 --> 00:02:59,960
has seized the human imagination for thousands of years.

51
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:02,300
In fact, the computer's family tree

52
00:03:02,400 --> 00:03:05,900
has roots so deep in the past, it is impossible to know

53
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:07,300
exactly where they begin.

54
00:03:14,840 --> 00:03:18,440
By the early 19th century, the European Industrial Revolution

55
00:03:18,540 --> 00:03:20,580
was well underway.

56
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:22,820
And the development in production and commerce

57
00:03:22,821 --> 00:03:26,720
and commerce came from the maturing fields of engineering,

58
00:03:26,820 --> 00:03:31,920
navigation, surveying, finance, and science.

59
00:03:32,020 --> 00:03:34,000
The practical application of these fields

60
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:37,000
relied on volume after volume of tables,

61
00:03:37,100 --> 00:03:40,540
tables for trigonometry, tides, interest rates,

62
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,040
multiplication, and gravity.

63
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:44,500
Tables were critical.

64
00:03:44,600 --> 00:03:47,120
The actual figuring was done by people who specialized

65
00:03:47,220 --> 00:03:49,320
in mathematical computation.

66
00:03:49,420 --> 00:03:52,820
Surprisingly, these people had a familiar job title.

67
00:03:52,821 --> 00:03:55,160
were called computers.

68
00:03:55,260 --> 00:03:58,720
These human computers toiled over their tables incessantly,

69
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:01,960
monotonously, and made mistakes.

70
00:04:02,060 --> 00:04:05,100
Typically, tables were full of errors.

71
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:07,140
The requirement for accurate tables

72
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:10,240
introduced one of the most eccentric and brilliant figures

73
00:04:10,340 --> 00:04:15,440
into the story of computers, Charles Babbage.

74
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:17,320
Oh, Babbage was an extraordinary scientist.

75
00:04:17,420 --> 00:04:18,740
I mean, Babbage was a great scientist.

76
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:21,780
Babbage was 100 years ahead of his time.

77
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:23,240
You can't say that about many people,

78
00:04:23,340 --> 00:04:25,220
but you could say that about Babbage.

79
00:04:25,320 --> 00:04:28,520
Charles Babbage represented that extraordinary element

80
00:04:28,620 --> 00:04:32,220
of British society, the scientist aristocrat.

81
00:04:32,320 --> 00:04:34,920
Many were known for their eccentricities,

82
00:04:35,020 --> 00:04:37,520
and Babbage was no exception.

83
00:04:37,620 --> 00:04:40,740
As a youth, Babbage devised footwear of hinged boards

84
00:04:40,840 --> 00:04:43,840
intended to allow him to walk on water.

85
00:04:43,940 --> 00:04:46,980
Never one to shirk adventure, he tried them out himself,

86
00:04:47,080 --> 00:04:49,640
but flipped over and nearly drowned.

87
00:04:49,641 --> 00:04:52,400
demonstrated his brilliance in mathematics while attending

88
00:04:52,500 --> 00:04:54,340
Trinity College in Cambridge.

89
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:58,140
In 1820, Babbage was checking the accuracy of calculations

90
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,720
made for the Royal Astronomical Society

91
00:05:00,820 --> 00:05:02,620
and kept finding errors.

92
00:05:02,720 --> 00:05:05,120
He reasoned that a machine could be constructed

93
00:05:05,220 --> 00:05:07,760
that would calculate the tables and directly

94
00:05:07,860 --> 00:05:09,200
print the results.

95
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:12,600
He called the machine the difference engine.

96
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:15,000
He drew up plans for a section of the device

97
00:05:15,100 --> 00:05:18,940
and had it built with his own funds in 1822.

98
00:05:18,941 --> 00:05:22,240
Babbage couldn't pay for the construction of the entire device,

99
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:23,880
but since the greatest beneficiaries

100
00:05:23,980 --> 00:05:26,020
would be the British government and people,

101
00:05:26,120 --> 00:05:28,380
he made the extraordinary step of petitioning

102
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:30,120
the government for a grant.

103
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:37,020
In 1823, the Treasury provided the project with startup funds.

104
00:05:37,120 --> 00:05:39,560
Government support for the computer industry is nothing

105
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:40,660
new.

106
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:43,360
It's very much a big topic in the news today,

107
00:05:43,460 --> 00:05:45,060
and it will continue to be.

108
00:05:45,160 --> 00:05:47,800
Computing is an expensive proposition,

109
00:05:47,801 --> 00:05:50,560
And it usually requires some government support

110
00:05:50,660 --> 00:05:52,100
if it's going to get anywhere.

111
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:54,500
Babbage hired a mechanical engineer

112
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:57,840
set to work on a complete design for the difference engine,

113
00:05:57,940 --> 00:06:00,840
and immediately ran into difficulties.

114
00:06:00,940 --> 00:06:02,780
The mechanical machine shops of the time

115
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:04,980
were not advanced enough to produce parts

116
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:08,380
in the precise measurements that Babbage's plans required.

117
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:10,760
So Babbage designed better machine tools,

118
00:06:10,860 --> 00:06:13,760
which would eventually improve the entire state of British

119
00:06:13,860 --> 00:06:16,020
tool manufacturing.

120
00:06:16,021 --> 00:06:19,720
By 1829, Babbage had spent the 1,500-pound grant

121
00:06:19,820 --> 00:06:22,120
from the government, and even more than that,

122
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:24,160
from his own funds.

123
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:26,560
But only a few bits and pieces of machine

124
00:06:26,660 --> 00:06:28,960
had been completed.

125
00:06:29,060 --> 00:06:33,580
Babbage's project began to attract critics.

126
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:36,940
He was plagued by several problems, one of his problems

127
00:06:37,040 --> 00:06:41,020
being his perfectionism, another problem being that his work

128
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:45,720
was not understood or appreciated by the people

129
00:06:45,721 --> 00:06:46,880
of his time.

130
00:06:46,980 --> 00:06:49,220
Babbage had many enemies.

131
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:52,160
Even London's organ grinders despised him

132
00:06:52,260 --> 00:06:54,960
because he had tried to have them banned as their music

133
00:06:55,060 --> 00:06:57,160
interfered with his thinking.

134
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:59,800
But the inventor continued to toil.

135
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:02,640
And finally, in 1832, there were enough parts

136
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:05,080
to assemble a section of the engine.

137
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:08,000
It functioned perfectly, solving equations

138
00:07:08,100 --> 00:07:10,540
and producing six-digit results.

139
00:07:10,640 --> 00:07:14,380
But it was only a small part of the proposed machine.

140
00:07:14,381 --> 00:07:17,280
The skyrocketing costs and lack of results

141
00:07:17,380 --> 00:07:19,660
finally made the government pull its support

142
00:07:19,760 --> 00:07:20,560
from the project.

143
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:24,920
Although disappointed by the cancellation,

144
00:07:25,020 --> 00:07:27,720
Babbage had contributed to the project's demise

145
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:30,500
by suggesting that a new device he had conceived,

146
00:07:30,600 --> 00:07:33,840
the analytical engine, would be vastly superior

147
00:07:33,940 --> 00:07:37,000
to his old design.

148
00:07:37,100 --> 00:07:41,000
Babbage, in hindsight, probably should have finished

149
00:07:41,001 --> 00:07:44,700
the difference engine and seen how far he could have gone

150
00:07:44,800 --> 00:07:48,240
with that before starting the analytical engine.

151
00:07:48,340 --> 00:07:50,240
There's no question that the analytical engine

152
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:53,740
was more than he could handle.

153
00:07:53,840 --> 00:07:56,440
Babbage was obsessed with his new idea.

154
00:07:56,540 --> 00:07:58,960
With the analytical engine, Babbage asked himself,

155
00:07:59,060 --> 00:08:00,560
why not build a machine that could

156
00:08:00,660 --> 00:08:03,260
solve any mathematical problem?

157
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:07,200
At the age of 43, Babbage had the vision of a computer,

158
00:08:07,300 --> 00:08:10,260
a vision he pursued for the rest of his life,

159
00:08:10,261 --> 00:08:13,960
The extraordinary fact is that Babbage's overall design

160
00:08:14,060 --> 00:08:17,600
for the analytical engine had many components analogous

161
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:20,300
to those in a modern computer.

162
00:08:20,400 --> 00:08:22,200
The heart of the machine, the mill,

163
00:08:22,300 --> 00:08:25,700
made the calculations, like the central processing unit

164
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:27,620
of modern computers.

165
00:08:27,720 --> 00:08:30,820
An oblong structure, the store held numbers

166
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:36,120
to be used in the calculations, like modern computer memory.

167
00:08:36,220 --> 00:08:39,060
Instructions and numbers could be fed into the machine

168
00:08:39,061 --> 00:08:41,360
using punch cards.

169
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:43,400
Much of what we know about the workings

170
00:08:43,500 --> 00:08:46,300
of the analytical engine came from the writings

171
00:08:46,400 --> 00:08:49,340
of Ada Countess of Lovelace.

172
00:08:49,440 --> 00:08:53,380
Among the people who understood what Babbage was doing

173
00:08:53,480 --> 00:08:58,940
was a woman named Ada Augusta, who was the daughter of,

174
00:08:59,040 --> 00:09:01,980
one of the daughters of Lord Byron, the poet.

175
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:05,560
She had studied mathematics as a child

176
00:09:05,660 --> 00:09:07,480
and had quite a bit of talent.

177
00:09:07,481 --> 00:09:10,480
Ada met Babbage at one of his famous dinner parties that

178
00:09:10,580 --> 00:09:13,460
were often attended by the luminaries of British science

179
00:09:13,560 --> 00:09:15,020
and engineering.

180
00:09:15,120 --> 00:09:18,120
Babbage demonstrated the working section of the difference

181
00:09:18,220 --> 00:09:22,100
engine for her, and she was immediately captivated by it.

182
00:09:22,200 --> 00:09:25,440
She published a simple description of Babbage's vision

183
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:28,940
for the analytical engine.

184
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:32,000
Ada wrote some descriptions of it,

185
00:09:32,100 --> 00:09:34,880
and she also appended to these descriptions

186
00:09:34,881 --> 00:09:40,120
a hypothetical way that this machine could solve an equation.

187
00:09:40,220 --> 00:09:42,620
And on the basis of those descriptions,

188
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:46,020
people often call her the world's first programmer.

189
00:09:46,120 --> 00:09:49,120
But she was never to program a real machine.

190
00:09:49,220 --> 00:09:51,360
As Babbage entered the last years of his life,

191
00:09:51,460 --> 00:09:53,800
his great work was unfinished.

192
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:56,640
He had become cranky and suffered constant attack

193
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:58,740
by his many enemies.

194
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,480
In 1871, when London's organ grinders

195
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:03,640
discovered that Babbage was ill, they

196
00:10:03,641 --> 00:10:06,140
They surrounded his house and serenaded him,

197
00:10:06,141 --> 00:10:09,340
increasing his agony until he died.

198
00:10:09,341 --> 00:10:11,880
Only a small portion of the analytical engine

199
00:10:11,881 --> 00:10:15,880
was built in Babbage's lifetime.

200
00:10:15,881 --> 00:10:19,660
Babbage's vision of the computer fell into obscurity,

201
00:10:19,661 --> 00:10:22,700
and except for the detailed texts left by Ada

202
00:10:22,701 --> 00:10:24,100
could well have been forgotten.

203
00:10:26,920 --> 00:10:29,840
Babbage's machines, which were never finished, which existed

204
00:10:29,841 --> 00:10:33,500
for the most part only on paper, were proto-computers.

205
00:10:33,501 --> 00:10:34,660
They were mechanical.

206
00:10:34,760 --> 00:10:35,560
They used gears.

207
00:10:35,660 --> 00:10:38,240
They used metal shafts.

208
00:10:38,340 --> 00:10:40,240
They weren't computers in our sense of the term.

209
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:42,740
That is, they weren't electronic digital computers.

210
00:10:42,840 --> 00:10:47,740
But they were abstractly and on paper mechanical computers.

211
00:10:47,840 --> 00:10:51,280
It would be nearly 100 years before a programmable

212
00:10:51,380 --> 00:10:54,180
computation device would again be conceived.

213
00:10:57,320 --> 00:10:59,700
Babbage predicted it would take just three years

214
00:10:59,800 --> 00:11:01,700
to complete the Difference Engine.

215
00:11:01,701 --> 00:11:03,200
It actually took him 14.

216
00:11:06,540 --> 00:11:08,740
In the second half of the 19th century,

217
00:11:08,840 --> 00:11:13,940
America's population increased 35% each decade.

218
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:16,240
America's exploding population began

219
00:11:16,340 --> 00:11:19,480
to endanger one of its great institutions,

220
00:11:19,580 --> 00:11:23,160
the American census.

221
00:11:23,260 --> 00:11:27,220
The census, which is required by the Constitution

222
00:11:27,221 --> 00:11:33,820
to be held every 10 years was still being done by old-fashioned

223
00:11:33,920 --> 00:11:37,760
people making check marks on pieces of paper.

224
00:11:37,860 --> 00:11:41,160
And it simply couldn't keep up with the tremendous surge

225
00:11:41,260 --> 00:11:44,200
of population in the US.

226
00:11:44,300 --> 00:11:47,800
The crisis reached ahead in 1887.

227
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:50,540
The Census Bureau was still hand-tallying the data

228
00:11:50,640 --> 00:11:52,920
from the 1880 census.

229
00:11:53,020 --> 00:11:54,980
Desperate for relief, the Bureau

230
00:11:54,981 --> 00:11:57,520
The Bureau pleaded for any method that could speed up

231
00:11:57,620 --> 00:12:00,720
the counting of the 1890 census.

232
00:12:00,820 --> 00:12:03,060
The superintendent of the census had proposals

233
00:12:03,160 --> 00:12:07,120
for three systems, so he decided to stage a contest.

234
00:12:07,220 --> 00:12:09,620
Two of the systems relied on hand counting.

235
00:12:09,720 --> 00:12:12,440
The third, developed by a young, rather humorless,

236
00:12:12,540 --> 00:12:15,600
former MIT instructor named Herman Hollerith,

237
00:12:15,700 --> 00:12:18,500
used punched cards.

238
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:21,380
Punch cards would one day become the standard method

239
00:12:21,381 --> 00:12:25,580
high volumes of data into computers.

240
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:29,520
Now, where he got this idea, we are not sure.

241
00:12:29,620 --> 00:12:32,660
He may have been inspired by the fact

242
00:12:32,760 --> 00:12:36,620
that a conductor on a railroad punches your ticket

243
00:12:36,720 --> 00:12:38,640
when you hand it to him.

244
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:41,200
Hollerith's system beat the others easily.

245
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:43,060
In the tabulation portion of the test,

246
00:12:43,160 --> 00:12:45,380
it was nearly 10 times faster.

247
00:12:45,480 --> 00:12:49,000
The Census Bureau leased 56 of Hollerith's machines

248
00:12:49,001 --> 00:12:54,600
at $1,000 a year each and put them to work in July 1890.

249
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:57,100
Census Bureau clerks used Holleritz machines

250
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:00,040
to punch the cards and then tabulate the results.

251
00:13:00,140 --> 00:13:03,140
Scores of operators were trained to use the puncher quickly

252
00:13:03,240 --> 00:13:05,240
and accurately.

253
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:08,160
The tabulating was done with electricity.

254
00:13:08,260 --> 00:13:10,360
A metal pin that passed through a card hole

255
00:13:10,460 --> 00:13:13,160
made electrical contact with a cup of mercury,

256
00:13:13,260 --> 00:13:16,500
completing a circuit that was registered on a tallying device

257
00:13:16,501 --> 00:13:20,700
that consisted of rows of clock-like dials.

258
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:23,800
Hollerith's machines were a step toward the later development

259
00:13:23,900 --> 00:13:25,100
of computers.

260
00:13:25,200 --> 00:13:29,180
They significantly sped up the processing of information.

261
00:13:29,280 --> 00:13:31,540
The results of the 1890 census count

262
00:13:31,640 --> 00:13:33,380
were a triumph for Hollerith.

263
00:13:33,480 --> 00:13:39,660
In just six weeks, the population count of 62,622,250

264
00:13:39,760 --> 00:13:41,260
was tallied.

265
00:13:41,360 --> 00:13:45,600
Hollerith became the talk of the scientific community.

266
00:13:45,601 --> 00:13:48,600
He rented an office and set himself up in business.

267
00:13:48,700 --> 00:13:53,240
He called his new enterprise the tabulating machine company.

268
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:56,340
Hollerith had the Census Bureau business in his pocket,

269
00:13:56,440 --> 00:13:58,080
and the future looked bright.

270
00:13:58,180 --> 00:14:00,580
But it turned out to be harder than it seemed.

271
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:11,820
Hollerith's natural aptitude for mechanical devices

272
00:14:11,920 --> 00:14:13,000
was obvious.

273
00:14:13,001 --> 00:14:16,640
But he also proved himself to be a dogged businessman.

274
00:14:16,740 --> 00:14:19,300
He drummed up business among one of the biggest industries

275
00:14:19,400 --> 00:14:21,640
of the day, the railroads.

276
00:14:21,740 --> 00:14:24,380
With the increase in population and the push west,

277
00:14:24,480 --> 00:14:28,120
the railroads had grown into enormous organizations,

278
00:14:28,220 --> 00:14:31,760
with personnel, stations, cars, and customers

279
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:34,360
scattered all across the country.

280
00:14:34,460 --> 00:14:37,560
Hundreds of clerks produced tons of paper

281
00:14:37,660 --> 00:14:41,060
to help track and manage these vast empires.

282
00:14:41,061 --> 00:14:43,300
Hollerith convinced the New York Central Railroad

283
00:14:43,400 --> 00:14:45,800
to try out some of his machines.

284
00:14:45,900 --> 00:14:48,700
The experiment wasn't a success.

285
00:14:48,800 --> 00:14:51,700
Hollerith's machines could compute fast enough for census

286
00:14:51,800 --> 00:14:54,480
work, but couldn't keep up with the speed and volume

287
00:14:54,580 --> 00:14:56,100
of the railroad business.

288
00:14:56,200 --> 00:14:59,620
After three months, the machines were removed.

289
00:14:59,720 --> 00:15:02,380
Hollerith was short on capital and faced ruin.

290
00:15:02,480 --> 00:15:05,560
He moved his family into his mother-in-law's house.

291
00:15:05,660 --> 00:15:07,560
He sold his assets, even his horse,

292
00:15:07,561 --> 00:15:10,400
to raise money to redesign his machines

293
00:15:10,500 --> 00:15:13,660
to improve their speed, reliability, and ability

294
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:15,260
to make additions.

295
00:15:15,360 --> 00:15:17,740
Hollerith even customized the punch cards

296
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:20,840
for business computations, such as adding columns

297
00:15:20,940 --> 00:15:22,880
to store dollars and cents.

298
00:15:22,980 --> 00:15:25,240
After a solid year of tedious work,

299
00:15:25,340 --> 00:15:27,240
Hollerith returned to New York Central

300
00:15:27,340 --> 00:15:30,280
and offered them free use of his new improved and faster

301
00:15:30,380 --> 00:15:32,460
computing machines for a year.

302
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:35,320
Within three months, the railroad was convinced

303
00:15:35,321 --> 00:15:37,820
and contracted to lease the machines.

304
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:42,820
The tabulating machine company was back on track.

305
00:15:42,920 --> 00:15:46,360
Hollerith had avoided bankruptcy and now had more work

306
00:15:46,460 --> 00:15:48,400
than he could handle.

307
00:15:48,500 --> 00:15:52,140
And Hollerith, you could say, came along just in time.

308
00:15:52,240 --> 00:15:57,040
It was a combination of his invention making this available,

309
00:15:57,140 --> 00:16:01,820
but also the need out there required something like that.

310
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:04,420
So it was a convergence of the social needs

311
00:16:04,421 --> 00:16:06,920
or the social factors on the one hand,

312
00:16:07,020 --> 00:16:09,920
and the inventiveness sort of pushing from the other hand.

313
00:16:10,020 --> 00:16:12,020
But Hollerith was weary.

314
00:16:12,120 --> 00:16:15,860
He was diagnosed with a bad heart and ordered to slow down.

315
00:16:15,960 --> 00:16:19,760
In 1911, Hollerith sold his shares in the tabulating machine

316
00:16:19,860 --> 00:16:22,680
company for over $1 million.

317
00:16:22,780 --> 00:16:26,480
Hollerith's former company was merged with three others

318
00:16:26,580 --> 00:16:29,220
and led by master salesman Thomas Watson

319
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:33,680
grew into a major supplier of business equipment.

320
00:16:33,681 --> 00:16:36,980
In 1924, Watson renamed the enterprise

321
00:16:37,080 --> 00:16:40,780
International Business Machines, IBM.

322
00:16:40,880 --> 00:16:43,220
Because of Hollerith, the name IBM

323
00:16:43,320 --> 00:16:47,320
would become synonymous with computers.

324
00:16:47,420 --> 00:16:51,440
By the 1930s, as America limped out of the Great Depression,

325
00:16:51,540 --> 00:16:56,040
companies like Burroughs and IBM foresaw continued growth

326
00:16:56,140 --> 00:16:58,440
and success.

327
00:16:58,540 --> 00:17:03,280
Over the next decade, progress would be slow.

328
00:17:03,281 --> 00:17:06,620
It would take the destructive forces of World War II

329
00:17:06,720 --> 00:17:09,520
to give the computer its next great advance.

330
00:17:12,420 --> 00:17:15,560
Herman Hollerith remarked, I will have in future years

331
00:17:15,660 --> 00:17:19,860
the satisfaction of being the first statistical engineer.

332
00:17:19,960 --> 00:17:21,860
He also had the satisfaction of becoming

333
00:17:21,960 --> 00:17:24,000
the first computer millionaire.

334
00:17:27,500 --> 00:17:31,100
World War II spurred the development of the true computer.

335
00:17:31,101 --> 00:17:40,900
And in the turbulent days before the German blitzkrieg smashed Poland, a young Polish engineer walked into the British embassy in Warsaw and made an astounding

336
00:17:40,901 --> 00:17:41,640
proposition.

337
00:17:42,340 --> 00:17:47,660
He offered to sell the British the secret to the unbreakable German code machine, the Enigma.

338
00:17:48,520 --> 00:17:56,740
The British desperately wanted to crack the Enigma machine used by German commanders to encrypt their most secret military radio messages.

339
00:17:56,741 --> 00:18:03,400
British intelligence supplied the engineer with a fake diplomatic passport and smuggled him out of

340
00:18:03,401 --> 00:18:04,101
Warsaw.

341
00:18:04,102 --> 00:18:10,780
While guarded by French agents in Paris, the engineer provided details on the code machine's

342
00:18:10,781 --> 00:18:12,247
ingenious operation.

343
00:18:12,248 --> 00:18:17,380
In the Enigma, plugs were rearranged to conform to that day's codebook

344
00:18:17,381 --> 00:18:18,217
combination.

345
00:18:18,218 --> 00:18:23,800
The power of Enigma was that this plug arrangement constantly varied how letters

346
00:18:23,801 --> 00:18:26,900
were coded throughout a transmission.

347
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:31,000
The number of letter variations was astronomical, so high,

348
00:18:31,100 --> 00:18:33,540
the Germans considered their code machine to be unbreakable.

349
00:18:36,740 --> 00:18:39,940
But the British now knew how the machine worked.

350
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:43,080
They realized that they could very quickly try different key

351
00:18:43,180 --> 00:18:45,780
combinations on a small part of the code.

352
00:18:45,880 --> 00:18:49,300
Then, when that small part was broken and the key revealed,

353
00:18:49,400 --> 00:18:51,400
the rest could be decoded effortlessly.

354
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:57,220
North of London at a secret installation in Bletchley Park,

355
00:18:57,680 --> 00:19:02,480
British codebreakers built a computer-like machine to do just that, Colossus.

356
00:19:03,300 --> 00:19:08,420
Colossus used over 2,000 vacuum tubes to process 25,000 characters per second.

357
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:12,540
Colossus could only do one job, but it could compute very quickly.

358
00:19:13,280 --> 00:19:16,380
Its deciphered German transmissions were called the Ultra Secret,

359
00:19:16,381 --> 00:19:18,720
the most closely guarded secret of the war.

360
00:19:19,320 --> 00:19:21,800
While the British were now able to read German messages,

361
00:19:21,801 --> 00:19:24,800
The question was how to take advantage of the secrets

362
00:19:24,801 --> 00:19:28,800
without tipping off the Germans that their code had been compromised.

363
00:19:28,801 --> 00:19:32,800
The most senior Allied commanders were privy to ultra material,

364
00:19:32,801 --> 00:19:37,800
but had to exercise caution in reacting to it so as not to tip their hand.

365
00:19:37,801 --> 00:19:42,800
Ultra information was never revealed to anyone in a position to be captured by the enemy.

366
00:19:42,801 --> 00:19:47,800
Field commanders often went into battle lacking information on the enemy

367
00:19:47,801 --> 00:19:50,800
that was known to their superiors from ultra dispatches.

368
00:19:50,801 --> 00:19:52,660
batches.

369
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:56,200
This secrecy may have also kept Colossus from prominence

370
00:19:56,300 --> 00:19:57,540
in the history of computers.

371
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:04,440
While Colossus was breaking German codes,

372
00:20:04,540 --> 00:20:07,480
across the Atlantic, another computational device

373
00:20:07,580 --> 00:20:09,480
was under construction.

374
00:20:09,580 --> 00:20:12,260
The machine, which would directly influence the design

375
00:20:12,360 --> 00:20:17,300
of all future computers, was being built in Philadelphia.

376
00:20:20,800 --> 00:20:32,340
In response to the attack on Pearl Harbor,

377
00:20:32,341 --> 00:20:35,740
American industry quickly became a powerhouse producer

378
00:20:35,741 --> 00:20:39,120
of the implements of war.

379
00:20:39,121 --> 00:20:41,820
But by 1943, there was a critical shortage

380
00:20:41,821 --> 00:20:44,560
of a surprising component of the war machine,

381
00:20:44,561 --> 00:20:48,620
firing tables for artillery pieces.

382
00:20:48,621 --> 00:20:51,920
Firing tables allowed gunners to correctly aim their guns

383
00:20:52,020 --> 00:20:54,720
in different ranges, altitudes, temperatures,

384
00:20:54,820 --> 00:20:56,420
and wind conditions.

385
00:20:56,520 --> 00:21:00,360
To calculate these tables required enormous numbers

386
00:21:00,460 --> 00:21:02,360
of calculations, which at that time

387
00:21:02,460 --> 00:21:04,760
were done by human beings.

388
00:21:04,860 --> 00:21:08,080
Incidentally, these people who did them were called computers.

389
00:21:08,180 --> 00:21:09,880
That was their job title.

390
00:21:09,980 --> 00:21:13,420
And they operated adding machines, mechanical adding

391
00:21:13,520 --> 00:21:14,980
machines primarily.

392
00:21:15,080 --> 00:21:17,580
And they would simply step through these calculations

393
00:21:17,581 --> 00:21:20,180
and produce these tables.

394
00:21:20,280 --> 00:21:22,680
One of the centers of firing table calculation

395
00:21:22,780 --> 00:21:24,580
was at the Moore School of Engineering

396
00:21:24,680 --> 00:21:27,720
at the University of Pennsylvania in Philadelphia.

397
00:21:27,820 --> 00:21:30,520
Midway through the war, it became clear that the tables

398
00:21:30,620 --> 00:21:33,100
could not be produced fast enough.

399
00:21:33,200 --> 00:21:35,440
This was a crisis.

400
00:21:35,540 --> 00:21:37,640
Without tables, new guns could not

401
00:21:37,740 --> 00:21:40,240
be shipped to the troops overseas.

402
00:21:40,340 --> 00:21:43,540
To break the bottleneck, a Moore School physicist, John

403
00:21:43,541 --> 00:21:48,140
John Mockley made a fantastic proposal.

404
00:21:48,240 --> 00:21:51,740
He suggested that he could build a giant electronic computer

405
00:21:51,840 --> 00:21:54,180
that would be able to figure a single trajectory

406
00:21:54,280 --> 00:21:56,880
in 100 seconds.

407
00:21:56,980 --> 00:22:00,420
The army, desperate for a device to help them win the war,

408
00:22:00,520 --> 00:22:03,000
reluctantly committed to the proposed cost

409
00:22:03,100 --> 00:22:06,460
of half a million dollars.

410
00:22:06,560 --> 00:22:08,440
Mockley and a brilliant graduate student

411
00:22:08,540 --> 00:22:10,800
in electrical engineering, Presper Eckert

412
00:22:10,801 --> 00:22:15,040
were constructing ENIAC, the electronic numeric integrator

413
00:22:15,140 --> 00:22:17,040
and computer.

414
00:22:17,140 --> 00:22:19,780
Driven by the knowledge that friends and relatives

415
00:22:19,880 --> 00:22:23,140
were dying in battle while they worked in Philadelphia,

416
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:27,020
the team of young engineers toiled incessantly.

417
00:22:27,120 --> 00:22:31,180
But could they create such a monster?

418
00:22:31,280 --> 00:22:34,960
Everything had to be invented from square one.

419
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:36,400
And then they had to build it.

420
00:22:36,500 --> 00:22:37,920
And then they had to test it.

421
00:22:38,020 --> 00:22:39,560
And they had to put it all together

422
00:22:39,561 --> 00:22:41,300
and make it work reliably.

423
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:43,560
And then they had to learn how to program it.

424
00:22:43,660 --> 00:22:47,400
Nothing close to ENIAC had ever been conceived.

425
00:22:47,500 --> 00:22:50,700
Nearly 100 feet long and weighing 30 tons,

426
00:22:50,800 --> 00:22:55,340
it contained almost 70,000 resistors, 10,000 capacitors,

427
00:22:55,440 --> 00:23:01,420
6,000 switches, and 18,000 delicate vacuum tubes.

428
00:23:01,520 --> 00:23:04,460
Vacuum tubes burn out just like light bulbs.

429
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:08,320
In a machine that contained 18,000 vacuum tubes,

430
00:23:08,321 --> 00:23:11,660
It was likely that at least one would always be burned out,

431
00:23:11,760 --> 00:23:13,260
crippling the machine.

432
00:23:13,360 --> 00:23:16,660
Presper Eckerd found the key to making ENIAC function.

433
00:23:16,760 --> 00:23:19,760
He had the vacuum tubes built to high tolerance.

434
00:23:19,860 --> 00:23:24,000
He critically tested them, and then he ran them at low power.

435
00:23:24,100 --> 00:23:26,240
If you took these measures, you

436
00:23:26,340 --> 00:23:28,640
might be able to get the machine to work for 10 minutes,

437
00:23:28,740 --> 00:23:30,940
half an hour at a time.

438
00:23:31,040 --> 00:23:33,240
Since the machine calculated so quickly,

439
00:23:33,340 --> 00:23:35,720
you can get a lot of work done in a half hour.

440
00:23:35,820 --> 00:23:37,220
And so that's what happened.

441
00:23:37,221 --> 00:23:42,220
After two years of intense work, ENIAC was complete a few months

442
00:23:42,320 --> 00:23:44,620
after the Japanese surrender.

443
00:23:44,720 --> 00:23:47,300
Although it wasn't finished in time to help win the war,

444
00:23:47,400 --> 00:23:49,700
ENIAC was a marvelous machine.

445
00:23:49,800 --> 00:23:53,400
Huge and hot, it could perform up to 5,000 additions,

446
00:23:53,500 --> 00:23:58,440
357 multiplications, and 38 divisions every second.

447
00:23:58,540 --> 00:24:02,040
By far and away the most complex machine of its time,

448
00:24:02,140 --> 00:24:04,720
ENIAC still lacked many of the qualities

449
00:24:04,820 --> 00:24:07,220
of a modern computer.

450
00:24:07,221 --> 00:24:09,620
Its memory was very primitive.

451
00:24:09,720 --> 00:24:13,760
It had to be laboriously rewired each time it was programmed

452
00:24:13,860 --> 00:24:15,660
and couldn't make logical decisions

453
00:24:15,760 --> 00:24:17,760
based on its calculations.

454
00:24:17,860 --> 00:24:20,660
But with tremendous expenditures of time and money,

455
00:24:20,760 --> 00:24:24,200
ENIAC had proved that computers could be constructed.

456
00:24:24,300 --> 00:24:27,840
However, except for arcane scientific calculation,

457
00:24:27,940 --> 00:24:29,840
did anyone really want them?

458
00:24:29,940 --> 00:24:31,480
The question lingered.

459
00:24:31,580 --> 00:24:35,140
Could anyone build a really practical computer?

460
00:24:37,220 --> 00:24:45,600
During development of the Mark II computer in 1945, a relay inside the computer failed

461
00:24:45,601 --> 00:24:48,840
and researchers found a dead moth inside.

462
00:24:48,841 --> 00:24:56,400
This is the origin of the computer terms bug and debugging.

463
00:24:56,401 --> 00:25:01,600
Just before the end of the Second World War, an advisor to the ENIAC project, John von

464
00:25:01,601 --> 00:25:07,200
Neumann, wrote a paper that was to greatly affect the next stage of computer design.

465
00:25:07,201 --> 00:25:09,900
Von Neumann possessed a photographic memory,

466
00:25:09,901 --> 00:25:12,400
an incomparably fast mind, and was one

467
00:25:12,401 --> 00:25:15,080
of the principal scientists involved in the Manhattan

468
00:25:15,081 --> 00:25:18,280
Project, the building of the atomic bomb.

469
00:25:18,281 --> 00:25:21,580
He was also an advisor on ENIAC.

470
00:25:21,581 --> 00:25:23,820
The paper Von Neumann wrote after the war

471
00:25:23,821 --> 00:25:26,680
delineated the structure of a modern computer.

472
00:25:26,681 --> 00:25:29,520
The paper drew heavily on the work building the ENIAC,

473
00:25:29,521 --> 00:25:34,960
yet was undeniably augmented by Von Neumann's brilliance.

474
00:25:34,961 --> 00:25:38,000
von Neumann's computer was to have a processing unit,

475
00:25:38,001 --> 00:25:41,960
a controlling unit, memory, input and output.

476
00:25:41,961 --> 00:25:45,540
But most importantly, in the evolution of computers,

477
00:25:45,541 --> 00:25:49,640
it would hold its programming internally in its memory.

478
00:25:49,641 --> 00:25:52,340
Internally held programs give computers

479
00:25:52,341 --> 00:25:56,140
their power and versatility, because an internal program

480
00:25:56,141 --> 00:25:58,420
can modify what it does based on data

481
00:25:58,421 --> 00:26:01,580
or the results of computations.

482
00:26:01,581 --> 00:26:03,960
In machines like the ENIAC, programming

483
00:26:03,961 --> 00:26:07,500
had been hardwired or fixed, so the machine was much less

484
00:26:07,501 --> 00:26:10,060
adaptable.

485
00:26:10,061 --> 00:26:12,520
The idea for storing the program internally

486
00:26:12,521 --> 00:26:15,960
was the last key to developing the true computer.

487
00:26:15,961 --> 00:26:17,960
But whether it was von Neumann's idea

488
00:26:17,961 --> 00:26:20,440
has long been hotly debated.

489
00:26:20,441 --> 00:26:23,740
Eckerd and Mockley claimed they formulated internal programs

490
00:26:23,741 --> 00:26:27,060
as a natural part of their work building ENIAC,

491
00:26:27,061 --> 00:26:29,460
although they couldn't stop and incorporate the idea

492
00:26:29,461 --> 00:26:31,080
into the machine.

493
00:26:31,081 --> 00:26:32,960
But many who read the paper assumed

494
00:26:32,961 --> 00:26:36,520
that all the genius behind it was from the great von Neumann.

495
00:26:36,521 --> 00:26:40,200
He was one of the most widely regarded mathematicians

496
00:26:40,201 --> 00:26:41,560
in the world.

497
00:26:41,561 --> 00:26:44,360
Eckerd and Mockley were relatively obscure.

498
00:26:44,361 --> 00:26:48,220
Eckerd was a young man just out of school.

499
00:26:48,221 --> 00:26:52,600
Mockley had been a professor at a fairly out of the way college.

500
00:26:52,601 --> 00:26:55,800
They didn't have international reputations.

501
00:26:55,801 --> 00:26:59,540
Eckerd and Mockley weren't included as authors in the paper.

502
00:26:59,541 --> 00:27:02,160
They felt they had been betrayed.

503
00:27:02,161 --> 00:27:04,700
The most important effect of von Neumann's paper

504
00:27:04,701 --> 00:27:07,400
was to spur computer development.

505
00:27:07,401 --> 00:27:10,740
Eckerd and Mockley moved into offices in Philadelphia,

506
00:27:10,741 --> 00:27:12,660
hired a staff, and set up a company

507
00:27:12,661 --> 00:27:14,600
that would build a business computer.

508
00:27:14,601 --> 00:27:16,740
They called it the UNIVAC.

509
00:27:16,741 --> 00:27:18,880
They signed a fixed cost contract

510
00:27:18,881 --> 00:27:21,720
to build a UNIVAC for the Census Bureau,

511
00:27:21,721 --> 00:27:24,880
rolled up their sleeves, and went to work.

512
00:27:24,881 --> 00:27:27,060
Unfortunately, building UNIVAC turned out

513
00:27:27,061 --> 00:27:30,060
to be a monumental undertaking.

514
00:27:30,061 --> 00:27:32,500
As they struggled to make UNIVAC real,

515
00:27:32,501 --> 00:27:35,940
they also struggled financially and needed to be bailed out

516
00:27:35,941 --> 00:27:38,740
by a series of larger companies.

517
00:27:38,741 --> 00:27:41,840
They eventually joined forces with Remington Rand,

518
00:27:41,841 --> 00:27:45,340
a flourishing typewriter manufacturer.

519
00:27:45,341 --> 00:27:49,480
In March of 1951, after six years of toil,

520
00:27:49,481 --> 00:27:51,780
they finally delivered the first UNIVAC

521
00:27:51,781 --> 00:27:53,420
to the Census Bureau.

522
00:27:53,421 --> 00:27:57,860
Unlike ENIAC, the UNIVAC was an entire computer system

523
00:27:57,861 --> 00:28:00,920
designed for business.

524
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:04,460
UNIVAC could be programmed for a variety of data processing

525
00:28:04,560 --> 00:28:05,760
tasks.

526
00:28:05,860 --> 00:28:08,380
Compact tape drives held data, and results were

527
00:28:08,480 --> 00:28:11,300
automatically printed.

528
00:28:11,400 --> 00:28:14,300
But even with the backing of Remington Rand's sales

529
00:28:14,400 --> 00:28:15,920
remained slow.

530
00:28:16,020 --> 00:28:21,420
Very few people understood how useful a computer could be.

531
00:28:21,520 --> 00:28:27,860
That perception changed dramatically one night in 1952.

532
00:28:27,861 --> 00:28:33,860
In a brilliant public relations move,

533
00:28:33,960 --> 00:28:37,680
Remington Rand arranged with CBS to use a UNIVAC on election

534
00:28:37,780 --> 00:28:40,340
night to predict the outcome of the presidential race

535
00:28:40,440 --> 00:28:43,840
between Dwight Eisenhower and Adlai Stevenson.

536
00:28:43,940 --> 00:28:46,920
Polls said the race was too close to call.

537
00:28:47,020 --> 00:28:49,040
No one had ever programmed a computer

538
00:28:49,140 --> 00:28:52,020
to make electoral predictions.

539
00:28:52,120 --> 00:28:54,080
Eckerd and Mockley's engineers entered

540
00:28:54,081 --> 00:28:58,820
their customized algorithms right until airtime.

541
00:28:58,821 --> 00:29:03,320
The operators fed in the results of selected Eastern precincts

542
00:29:03,321 --> 00:29:05,960
and at 9 PM ran their program.

543
00:29:05,961 --> 00:29:09,080
UNIVAC predicted a landslide for Eisenhower,

544
00:29:09,081 --> 00:29:11,360
but the poll said differently.

545
00:29:11,361 --> 00:29:14,320
The operators didn't believe what UNIVAC was telling them

546
00:29:14,321 --> 00:29:17,220
and assumed their programming was at fault.

547
00:29:17,221 --> 00:29:19,480
Quickly, they reprogrammed the machine

548
00:29:19,481 --> 00:29:22,360
to better reflect what the experts predicted.

549
00:29:22,361 --> 00:29:24,120
As the night went on, it became clear

550
00:29:24,121 --> 00:29:27,580
that Eisenhower would indeed win by a landslide.

551
00:29:27,581 --> 00:29:33,080
CBS sheepishly announced they hadn't believed the machines.

552
00:29:33,081 --> 00:29:35,820
When all the votes were tallied, UNIVAC's initial prediction

553
00:29:35,821 --> 00:29:40,060
was off by less than 1% of the final result,

554
00:29:40,061 --> 00:29:43,860
an extraordinary prediction even by today's standards.

555
00:29:43,861 --> 00:29:47,360
The power and utility of the computer had been proved.

556
00:29:52,360 --> 00:29:57,360
After the success of UNIVAC, various companies

557
00:29:57,460 --> 00:30:01,240
began to see a future in computer development.

558
00:30:01,340 --> 00:30:04,440
New companies like Burroughs, as well as old giants

559
00:30:04,540 --> 00:30:07,940
like General Electric, jumped into the computer business.

560
00:30:08,040 --> 00:30:10,080
But most large American businesses

561
00:30:10,180 --> 00:30:12,640
were dependent on the data processing systems

562
00:30:12,740 --> 00:30:17,660
provided by one company, the office machine monolith, IBM.

563
00:30:17,661 --> 00:30:24,660
IBM had no computers.

564
00:30:24,760 --> 00:30:28,180
IBM's aging leader, the legendary sales guru,

565
00:30:28,280 --> 00:30:31,180
Thomas Watson, Sr., was not eager to jump

566
00:30:31,280 --> 00:30:36,140
into the enormously costly development of computers.

567
00:30:36,240 --> 00:30:39,920
A computer back then contained thousands of vacuum tubes,

568
00:30:40,020 --> 00:30:43,920
occupied one or more large rooms,

569
00:30:43,921 --> 00:30:48,560
and required a small army of attendants to run.

570
00:30:48,660 --> 00:30:52,260
IBM and most other people didn't see how computers

571
00:30:52,360 --> 00:30:53,860
could be used in business.

572
00:30:53,960 --> 00:30:56,760
It took Watson's son, Tom Watson, Jr.,

573
00:30:56,860 --> 00:31:00,240
to lead IBM into the computer age.

574
00:31:00,340 --> 00:31:02,800
As Watson himself recalls, the move

575
00:31:02,900 --> 00:31:05,800
was spurred by IBM's customers who were fed up

576
00:31:05,900 --> 00:31:07,580
with bulky punch cards.

577
00:31:07,680 --> 00:31:09,680
I remember particularly Jim Madden,

578
00:31:09,780 --> 00:31:12,920
then vice president of the Metropolitan Life,

579
00:31:12,921 --> 00:31:16,020
who said, we're going to cancel our IBM just the minute

580
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:18,700
we learned to do this in tapes, because three floors

581
00:31:18,800 --> 00:31:20,800
of the Metropolitan Life building

582
00:31:20,900 --> 00:31:24,160
are used to store the cards of our customers' accounts.

583
00:31:24,260 --> 00:31:26,700
And if we keep going the way we will,

584
00:31:26,800 --> 00:31:28,540
they'll occupy the whole building.

585
00:31:28,640 --> 00:31:32,640
We were threatened into this progress.

586
00:31:32,740 --> 00:31:35,140
Faced with the prospect of losing customers,

587
00:31:35,240 --> 00:31:39,220
Tom Watson, Jr. ordered the development of a computer.

588
00:31:39,320 --> 00:31:42,120
IBM's famous sales force told their customers

589
00:31:42,121 --> 00:31:45,000
customers, a computer would arrive soon.

590
00:31:45,001 --> 00:31:46,980
In fact, it was still being planned.

591
00:31:46,981 --> 00:31:52,300
Finally, in 1953, IBM unveiled the 701.

592
00:31:52,301 --> 00:31:55,440
Although technologically inferior to the UNIVAC,

593
00:31:55,441 --> 00:31:58,180
the 701 and other early IBM computers

594
00:31:58,181 --> 00:32:01,020
were hits with the customers because they conformed

595
00:32:01,021 --> 00:32:05,160
to standard IBM systems and support.

596
00:32:05,161 --> 00:32:08,980
IBM saw the future, and it was computers.

597
00:32:08,981 --> 00:32:12,580
They redirected corporate efforts to computer development.

598
00:32:12,581 --> 00:32:16,480
IBM's efforts paid off, and by the beginning of the 1960s,

599
00:32:16,481 --> 00:32:19,000
the company's large mainframe computers

600
00:32:19,001 --> 00:32:21,280
dominated the business.

601
00:32:21,281 --> 00:32:22,660
But the development of the computer

602
00:32:22,661 --> 00:32:26,360
was about to go in an altogether different direction.

603
00:32:38,980 --> 00:32:43,980
We choose to go to the moon in this decade

604
00:32:44,080 --> 00:32:47,720
and do the other things, not because they are easy,

605
00:32:47,820 --> 00:32:49,620
but because they are hard.

606
00:32:49,720 --> 00:32:53,060
In 1961, America was trailing Russia

607
00:32:53,160 --> 00:32:54,660
in the race for space.

608
00:33:00,240 --> 00:33:04,580
1961, a year of achievement for Soviet scientists

609
00:33:04,680 --> 00:33:05,780
in the race for space.

610
00:33:08,980 --> 00:33:13,220
Yuri Gagarin has become the first human to orbit the Earth,

611
00:33:13,320 --> 00:33:15,520
and crowds in Moscow's Red Square

612
00:33:15,620 --> 00:33:18,020
salute the 27-year-old cosmonaut.

613
00:33:18,120 --> 00:33:21,420
As NASA engineers began planning the lunar mission,

614
00:33:21,520 --> 00:33:24,920
they realized a computer as powerful as one currently

615
00:33:25,020 --> 00:33:28,440
the size of a room must be on board.

616
00:33:28,540 --> 00:33:32,640
The engineers wondered, is such a small computer even possible?

617
00:33:32,740 --> 00:33:34,680
The first great breakthrough that would lead

618
00:33:34,780 --> 00:33:37,100
to computer miniaturization had already

619
00:33:37,101 --> 00:33:41,440
had already been made on December 23, 1947,

620
00:33:41,540 --> 00:33:45,840
when three scientists at Bell Labs, William Shockley, Walter

621
00:33:45,940 --> 00:33:49,940
Bertain, and John Bardeen invented the transistor.

622
00:33:50,040 --> 00:33:52,240
Formed on the semiconductor silicon,

623
00:33:52,340 --> 00:33:56,960
the transistor could replace large vacuum tubes in computers.

624
00:33:57,060 --> 00:34:00,120
Compared to vacuum tubes, transistors were tiny,

625
00:34:00,220 --> 00:34:03,800
required little power, and produced little heat.

626
00:34:03,900 --> 00:34:05,820
The breakthrough was sufficiently important

627
00:34:05,821 --> 00:34:10,527
that the three inventors of the transistor were awarded the Nobel Prize.

628
00:34:10,528 --> 00:34:11,900
A computer that could

629
00:34:11,901 --> 00:34:17,260
navigate to the moon and back would require thousands of transistors, and although small,

630
00:34:17,261 --> 00:34:19,753
they were not nearly small enough.

631
00:34:19,754 --> 00:34:23,420
The next step in miniaturization occurred in 1959,

632
00:34:23,421 --> 00:34:28,540
when Robert Noyce and Jack Kilby, engineers for rival transistor manufacturers,

633
00:34:28,541 --> 00:34:33,100
independently came up with breakthroughs that led to the same revolutionary idea.

634
00:34:33,101 --> 00:34:37,100
an entire network of electronic components, transistors,

635
00:34:37,200 --> 00:34:39,100
diodes, capacitors, and resistors

636
00:34:39,200 --> 00:34:45,040
could be incorporated onto a single chip of silicon.

637
00:34:45,140 --> 00:34:47,040
The great innovation in electronics

638
00:34:47,140 --> 00:34:49,540
was called the integrated circuit.

639
00:34:49,640 --> 00:34:53,060
Using integrated circuits, a 10-ounce computer

640
00:34:53,160 --> 00:34:56,860
was built that was as powerful as a 30-pound one made

641
00:34:56,960 --> 00:34:58,860
of transistors.

642
00:34:58,960 --> 00:35:02,100
But integrated circuits had an inherent problem.

643
00:35:02,101 --> 00:35:04,740
They were difficult to manufacture and therefore

644
00:35:04,840 --> 00:35:06,040
were expensive.

645
00:35:06,140 --> 00:35:11,540
But the space race had started just in time to pay for them.

646
00:35:11,640 --> 00:35:15,040
In 1969, 5,000 integrated circuits

647
00:35:15,140 --> 00:35:18,620
made up the heart of each of two identical computers,

648
00:35:18,720 --> 00:35:23,320
one on the lunar orbiter and one on the lander.

649
00:35:23,420 --> 00:35:27,100
For their size, these were the most powerful computers

650
00:35:27,200 --> 00:35:32,100
on Earth, soon to leave the Earth entirely.

651
00:35:32,101 --> 00:35:33,600
Beautiful, just beautiful.

652
00:35:36,940 --> 00:35:39,440
I'm going to step off the land now.

653
00:35:39,540 --> 00:35:43,840
As Neil Armstrong took one small step in the lunar dust,

654
00:35:43,940 --> 00:35:47,620
Intel engineer Ted Hoff was making the last great leap

655
00:35:47,720 --> 00:35:51,080
in miniaturization, developing an idea that

656
00:35:51,180 --> 00:35:54,720
would put an entire computer on a chip of silicon,

657
00:35:54,820 --> 00:35:56,860
the microprocessor.

658
00:35:56,960 --> 00:35:58,820
The genie would be out of its bottle,

659
00:35:58,920 --> 00:36:00,920
beginning the computer revolution

660
00:36:00,921 --> 00:36:03,060
and changing the world forever.

661
00:36:08,620 --> 00:36:13,160
IBM took the lead in computer sales in 1956 from Remington

662
00:36:13,260 --> 00:36:16,060
Rand by selling just 76 computers.

663
00:36:21,180 --> 00:36:23,480
OK, here's the way that Apollo here.

664
00:36:23,580 --> 00:36:26,680
Apollo 13, intended as the third lunar landing,

665
00:36:26,780 --> 00:36:30,220
has just lost two fuel cells and was venting oxygen

666
00:36:30,221 --> 00:36:34,660
into space, 200,000 miles from Earth.

667
00:36:34,760 --> 00:36:38,360
Soon, the second oxygen tank would begin losing pressure.

668
00:36:38,460 --> 00:36:41,600
The astronauts would die unless they could precisely align

669
00:36:41,700 --> 00:36:45,740
their spacecraft and fire their rocket to slingshot themselves

670
00:36:45,840 --> 00:36:48,600
around the moon and back to Earth.

671
00:36:48,700 --> 00:36:51,540
Critical to the alignment was Apollo's state-of-the-art

672
00:36:51,640 --> 00:36:53,640
guidance computer.

673
00:36:53,740 --> 00:36:55,380
A new trajectory was figured.

674
00:36:55,480 --> 00:36:58,040
The all-or-nothing rocket firing was made.

675
00:36:58,041 --> 00:37:01,140
The Apollo 13 crew miraculously returned to Earth

676
00:37:01,240 --> 00:37:04,780
with the help of their small and powerful computer.

677
00:37:04,880 --> 00:37:07,620
By the last Apollo mission, two and a half years later,

678
00:37:07,720 --> 00:37:10,220
computers as powerful as those on Apollo

679
00:37:10,320 --> 00:37:12,760
would be available to everyone.

680
00:37:12,860 --> 00:37:15,520
That was because soon before the Apollo 13 flight,

681
00:37:15,620 --> 00:37:18,520
an engineer at Intel, Ted Hoff, had come up

682
00:37:18,620 --> 00:37:20,800
with an ingenious idea.

683
00:37:20,900 --> 00:37:24,040
Hoff had been told to design 12 separate integrated circuits

684
00:37:24,140 --> 00:37:26,340
to make a Japanese pocket calculator.

685
00:37:26,341 --> 00:37:29,380
He suggested placing the entire processing unit on a chip

686
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:31,880
and programming it just like a computer.

687
00:37:31,980 --> 00:37:35,020
Intel developed the idea, and by 1970,

688
00:37:35,120 --> 00:37:39,960
they had a working model of a microprocessor.

689
00:37:40,060 --> 00:37:44,900
It was the invention not just of integrated circuits,

690
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:46,900
but of a particular kind of integrated circuit,

691
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:50,440
the microprocessor that makes today's personal computers

692
00:37:50,540 --> 00:37:51,440
possible.

693
00:37:51,540 --> 00:37:54,160
Smaller than a fingernail, a microprocessor

694
00:37:54,161 --> 00:37:56,560
contains many of the components of the computer,

695
00:37:56,660 --> 00:38:00,760
including a control unit, a clock, and areas where data

696
00:38:00,860 --> 00:38:03,300
can be stored and modified.

697
00:38:03,400 --> 00:38:06,500
Processing power was about to become very cheap

698
00:38:06,600 --> 00:38:10,000
and very compact.

699
00:38:10,100 --> 00:38:14,420
In the mid-1970s, two friends, Stephen Wozniak and Steve Jobs,

700
00:38:14,520 --> 00:38:20,020
were manufacturing a small computer in a Palo Alto garage.

701
00:38:20,120 --> 00:38:23,920
Steve Jobs was a college dropout.

702
00:38:23,921 --> 00:38:25,660
But he was a college dropout with a difference.

703
00:38:25,760 --> 00:38:27,660
He was very intelligent.

704
00:38:27,760 --> 00:38:29,520
He had a lot of street smarts.

705
00:38:29,620 --> 00:38:31,960
He was also extremely ambitious.

706
00:38:32,060 --> 00:38:34,160
I think perhaps more important than that,

707
00:38:34,260 --> 00:38:37,560
he knew the other Steve, Steve Wozniak,

708
00:38:37,660 --> 00:38:39,760
had created something exceptional.

709
00:38:39,860 --> 00:38:41,980
Steve Jobs sold his Volkswagen,

710
00:38:42,080 --> 00:38:44,680
and Steven Wozniak sold his HP calculator

711
00:38:44,780 --> 00:38:47,820
to finance their company that would revolutionize

712
00:38:47,920 --> 00:38:49,720
the computer industry.

713
00:38:49,820 --> 00:38:52,720
Jobs trekked all over the San Francisco Bay Area

714
00:38:52,721 --> 00:38:56,160
to find buyers for the $500 machine, which they called

715
00:38:56,260 --> 00:38:58,660
the Apple I.

716
00:38:58,760 --> 00:39:01,220
The Apple I was large and unwieldy.

717
00:39:01,320 --> 00:39:04,100
Jobs realized they needed a new design for a computer

718
00:39:04,200 --> 00:39:05,760
that anyone could use.

719
00:39:05,860 --> 00:39:09,600
Wozniak began to build the Apple II.

720
00:39:09,700 --> 00:39:11,900
The fate of Apple changed dramatically

721
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:16,580
when in the fall of 1976, a visitor to Wozniak's garage

722
00:39:16,680 --> 00:39:19,420
saw the prototype of the Apple II.

723
00:39:19,520 --> 00:39:22,720
The visitor was Mike Markula, who at 32 had retired

724
00:39:22,721 --> 00:39:24,760
from Intel a millionaire.

725
00:39:24,860 --> 00:39:28,100
He was so impressed by the Apple II that he joined Apple

726
00:39:28,200 --> 00:39:30,900
and put it on a sound business footing.

727
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:33,160
The Apple II was introduced to the public

728
00:39:33,260 --> 00:39:34,860
and sales skyrocketed.

729
00:39:34,960 --> 00:39:37,900
But in 1978, even with the success of the Apple II,

730
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:41,080
using a computer wasn't easy.

731
00:39:41,180 --> 00:39:45,300
The early Apple computers, as well as all early personal

732
00:39:45,400 --> 00:39:50,420
computers, did not have graphical interfaces.

733
00:39:50,421 --> 00:39:53,760
They didn't have mice.

734
00:39:53,860 --> 00:39:58,100
They had what's known as a command line interface.

735
00:39:58,200 --> 00:40:01,160
That is, you typed instructions into the computer,

736
00:40:01,260 --> 00:40:04,300
and your instructions appeared as text on the screen.

737
00:40:07,740 --> 00:40:10,040
A simple-to-use computer had been conceived

738
00:40:10,140 --> 00:40:13,640
by a computer scientist named Doug Engelbart.

739
00:40:13,740 --> 00:40:17,820
He demonstrated his vision in 1968 at the Fall Joint Computer

740
00:40:17,920 --> 00:40:20,420
Conference in San Francisco.

741
00:40:20,421 --> 00:40:24,060
W, it'll say delete word.

742
00:40:24,160 --> 00:40:27,620
The arrow moves back and forth to give me feedback.

743
00:40:27,720 --> 00:40:30,060
Wielding a keyboard and a pointing device he called a

744
00:40:30,160 --> 00:40:33,300
mouse, Engelbart worked with a computer 30 miles away,

745
00:40:33,400 --> 00:40:37,040
linked by microwaves, and demonstrated word processing

746
00:40:37,140 --> 00:40:38,740
and hypertext.

747
00:40:38,840 --> 00:40:41,240
Many in the audience went home inspired,

748
00:40:41,340 --> 00:40:44,920
but one group alone was to fulfill Engelbart's vision.

749
00:40:45,020 --> 00:40:48,420
That group was just down the road from San Francisco

750
00:40:48,421 --> 00:40:49,320
at Xerox PARC.

751
00:40:52,620 --> 00:40:55,820
In 1970, Xerox dominated the copier industry,

752
00:40:55,821 --> 00:40:59,200
but thought the future might be in computers.

753
00:40:59,201 --> 00:41:01,800
A young Xerox executive, Robert Taylor,

754
00:41:01,801 --> 00:41:04,940
worked with a team to transform the way the computer industry

755
00:41:04,941 --> 00:41:09,740
was perceived, as Robert Taylor himself remembers.

756
00:41:09,741 --> 00:41:13,240
The chairman of Xerox at the time, Peter McCullough,

757
00:41:13,241 --> 00:41:17,640
made a speech where he said that Xerox was going

758
00:41:17,641 --> 00:41:20,680
to become the architecture of information.

759
00:41:20,681 --> 00:41:24,080
So I asked his speechwriter not too long after that,

760
00:41:24,081 --> 00:41:25,240
what did that mean?

761
00:41:26,380 --> 00:41:29,420
Because I had an idea about what it ought to mean,

762
00:41:29,421 --> 00:41:30,660
and so did some other people.

763
00:41:30,661 --> 00:41:33,360
And the speechwriter said, well, he didn't really know,

764
00:41:33,361 --> 00:41:35,700
but it's a ringing phrase.

765
00:41:35,701 --> 00:41:39,020
And so I said, well, we're gonna make it happen.

766
00:41:39,021 --> 00:41:41,100
Robert Taylor hired many of the country's

767
00:41:41,101 --> 00:41:44,040
top computer scientists and challenged them

768
00:41:44,041 --> 00:41:47,300
to create an easy to use personal computer.

769
00:41:47,301 --> 00:41:52,200
The result was the Alto, which incorporated many of the innovations in personal computers

770
00:41:52,201 --> 00:41:57,540
we take for granted today, all developed at Xerox PARC.

771
00:41:57,541 --> 00:42:03,400
The Alto used a mouse, a graphical interface, built-in networking and printed on a laser

772
00:42:03,401 --> 00:42:04,400
printer.

773
00:42:04,401 --> 00:42:10,200
Xerox developed the star, the commercial model of the Alto, but it never sold well.

774
00:42:10,201 --> 00:42:17,200
Xerox was a large and very successful photocopier company.

775
00:42:17,300 --> 00:42:20,280
And it didn't really understand computers.

776
00:42:20,380 --> 00:42:26,120
It didn't appreciate the brilliance, the originality,

777
00:42:26,220 --> 00:42:32,720
and the enormous commercial worth of the computer

778
00:42:32,820 --> 00:42:34,320
developments at Xerox PARC.

779
00:42:34,420 --> 00:42:38,720
But Steve Jobs did when he visited Xerox PARC in 1979

780
00:42:38,721 --> 00:42:40,720
and saw the Alto.

781
00:42:40,721 --> 00:42:42,720
He returned to Apple and immediately set to work

782
00:42:42,721 --> 00:42:45,720
on what would become the Macintosh computer,

783
00:42:45,721 --> 00:42:47,720
the first popular personal computer

784
00:42:47,721 --> 00:42:49,720
similar to those used today.

785
00:42:49,721 --> 00:42:51,720
What made the Macintosh easy to use

786
00:42:51,721 --> 00:42:54,720
was its operating system and applications,

787
00:42:54,721 --> 00:42:56,720
otherwise known as software.

788
00:42:56,721 --> 00:42:58,720
Increasingly, software was dominating

789
00:42:58,721 --> 00:43:00,720
the advances made in computers.

790
00:43:00,721 --> 00:43:02,720
Bill Gates, the young president

791
00:43:02,721 --> 00:43:04,720
of a computing software company, Microsoft,

792
00:43:04,721 --> 00:43:06,720
understood the importance of software

793
00:43:06,721 --> 00:43:09,060
of software to the future of computers,

794
00:43:09,160 --> 00:43:12,620
and parlayed this vision into a vast software empire,

795
00:43:12,720 --> 00:43:16,100
making him the richest man in the world.

796
00:43:16,200 --> 00:43:18,000
The dream of a machine that could think

797
00:43:18,100 --> 00:43:21,040
had come from a mechanical device to an electronic one,

798
00:43:21,140 --> 00:43:23,540
where the bits of coded information that ran it

799
00:43:23,640 --> 00:43:26,040
were as important, perhaps more important,

800
00:43:26,140 --> 00:43:28,580
than the machine that they controlled.

801
00:43:28,680 --> 00:43:30,280
This may have been the best evidence

802
00:43:30,380 --> 00:43:32,840
that a thinking machine had arrived,

803
00:43:32,940 --> 00:43:35,920
and could now be placed on your desk.

804
00:43:35,921 --> 00:43:40,300
Soon, nearly half the jobs in America would use the computer.

805
00:43:40,400 --> 00:43:42,800
Within the decade, microprocessors

806
00:43:42,900 --> 00:43:47,540
would be everywhere, incorporated into automobiles, appliances,

807
00:43:47,640 --> 00:43:51,540
and scientific instruments, significantly increasing

808
00:43:51,640 --> 00:43:55,940
their capability and reliability.

809
00:43:56,040 --> 00:43:59,540
Unprecedented fortunes would be made in businesses that hadn't

810
00:43:59,640 --> 00:44:01,680
existed a decade before.

811
00:44:01,780 --> 00:44:04,340
Education would be transformed forever,

812
00:44:04,341 --> 00:44:06,900
As the access to information would be transferred

813
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:10,180
from libraries and universities to your desktop,

814
00:44:10,280 --> 00:44:12,780
the Earth would shrink with unprecedented speed

815
00:44:12,880 --> 00:44:16,920
as a worldwide communication grid became accessible to anyone

816
00:44:17,020 --> 00:44:20,420
with a computer and modem.

817
00:44:20,520 --> 00:44:23,620
And even now, the evolution of a thinking machine

818
00:44:23,720 --> 00:44:25,120
isn't finished.

819
00:44:25,220 --> 00:44:28,240
Computers have already changed the way we live,

820
00:44:28,340 --> 00:44:32,100
and they'll change the way we explore our world and other

821
00:44:32,101 --> 00:44:34,600
in the 21st century.

822
00:44:34,700 --> 00:44:36,980
They will take us to distant galaxies,

823
00:44:37,080 --> 00:44:39,980
and they will connect us right here on Earth.

824
00:44:40,080 --> 00:44:43,620
Computers will continue to become more interconnected.

825
00:44:43,720 --> 00:44:48,180
They will continue to become smaller, faster, cheaper,

826
00:44:48,280 --> 00:44:50,620
and software will become more powerful.

827
00:44:53,980 --> 00:44:57,060
Minds that have yet to be formed will mold this power

828
00:44:57,160 --> 00:45:02,100
to create new marvels inconceivable to us today.

829
00:46:02,100 --> 00:46:04,100
You

